From: x6100-q3 <x6100-q3@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: sunxi: keep the IRQ function on direction_input of an IRQ line

A pin delivering an interrupt is muxed to the "irq" function when the
interrupt is requested. gpio_direction_input() on that line switched it
to the plain GPIO input function, and nothing switched it back: the line
still read correctly but raised no more interrupts.

gpio-matrix-keypad does exactly that, on every row, at every scan (rows
are its interrupt lines). On the Xiegu X6100 (R16), the first scan,
when the keypad is opened, left all five rows without interrupts and no
key was ever reported again; the rotary encoders on the same controller
kept working.

The IRQ function is an input, so treat the request as done. Reading the
level in IRQ mode is unchanged (irq_read_needs_mux covers the SoCs that
need the input function for that).

---
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c	2026-10-26 00:14:52
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c	2026-10-26 00:14:52
@@ -955,6 +955,14 @@
 					unsigned offset)
 {
 	struct sunxi_pinctrl *pctl = gpiochip_get_data(chip);
+
+	/*
+	 * A line used as an interrupt is muxed to the IRQ function, which is
+	 * an input already. Switching it to the plain GPIO input function
+	 * would silently stop its interrupts until it is requested again.
+	 */
+	if (gpiochip_line_is_irq(chip, offset))
+		return 0;
 
 	return sunxi_pmx_gpio_set_direction(pctl->pctl_dev, NULL,
 					    chip->base + offset, true);
